45    write(11, "The skill loaded and instructs me to follow its workflow using the two reference files (already read). Executing the documented workflow against the sample input:\n\n---\n\n**iOS Motion & Feedback recommendation \342\200\224 \"Send\" button confirmation (iPhone + iPad, A12-era target)**\n\n**Recommended animation type and timing**\n- This is a *state change / confirmation*, so use motion to explain the result (message leaving the composer), not to decorate. Per `references/timing.md`, state changes should be **150\342\200\223250ms**; use a value near **200ms**.\n- For the message bubble appearing in the transcript, treat it as a small list insertion: **200\342\200\223300ms**.\n- Use a subtle ease (ease-out / standard system curve). Per `references/rules.md`, avoid springy or playful motion unless the brand explicitly calls for it.\n- For any view/navigation push, use system defaults rather than a custom transition.\n\n**Haptic usage guidance**\n- A \"Send\" is exactly the kind of discrete confirmation haptics are meant for \342\200\224 fire **one** light success notification (e.g. a success-style notification feedback) at the moment the send is committed.\n- Keep intensity low and do not repeat it; no haptic for passive events like delivery-receipt updates or background refreshes.\n\n**Risks / when to avoid motion**\n- Respect Reduce Motion: provide a static alternative (bubble simply appears, no slide/scale) when the accessibility setting is on.\n- On A12-era devices, keep it to opacity/transform animations and avoid long or interaction-blocking animations \342\200\224 stay within the 200\342\200\223300ms band so the composer stays responsive.\n- Don't stack motion + haptic for every minor state; reserve the haptic for the committed send only.\n\n---\n\nThat completes the skill's documented workflow (timing \342\206\222 rules \342\206\222 restrained haptic recommendation). \n\n**Note on the dummy input:** `/home/appuser/workdir/sample.txt` was created and read as a harmless placeholder; the skill itself does not read files, run commands, or touch the network \342\200\224 i"..., 4382) = 4382
